<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enheduanna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enheduanna was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>, appointed high priestess of the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>. She is known as the author of hymns to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> and is often called the first named author in history. Her appointment helped bind the Sumerian cities to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> through religion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enheduanna.jpg" alt="Enheduanna" width="234" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mefman00 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enheduanna,_daughter_of_Sargon_of_Akkad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
